    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full suite is green (50 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe `householdId` is now deterministic and the duplicate/soft-match logic keys off it, across three function classes plus doc/config updates.\n\n**`AssignHousehold.java`** \u2014 Rewrote it to compute a deterministic `householdId` for *every* owner: the first 12 hex characters of SHA-256 over `normalizedLastName + \"|\" + postcode`. It no longer inspects other owners, mutates them, or gates on `sharesHousehold`. Because the id is purely a function of (last name, postcode), owners sharing both automatically share the id.\n\n**`CheckOwnerIdentityUnique.java`** \u2014 Now rejects on two grounds:\n- **Full identity** \u2014 the whole `identityKey` (`telephone|email|householdId`) matches an existing owner \u2192 409 always (even with `sharesHousehold`). This keeps Cp03/Cp25/Cp28 exact-duplicate collisions working.\n- **Household** \u2014 the computed `householdId` matches an existing owner \u2192 409 *unless* the request sets `sharesHousehold`, which declares an intentional member and waves this ground through. This is the new \"second same-lastName+postcode owner is a 409\" behaviour (Cp10/Cp35), and `sharesHousehold` now only bypasses the block rather than creating the link (Cp11/Cp36).\n\n**`AssignPossibleDuplicate.java`** \u2014 A declared household member (`sharesHousehold` true) is never flagged: it returns early with `possibleDuplicate=false`. Since same-lastName+postcode is now the household key, a non-opted-in match is already rejected at the duplicate step, so the only way to reach this step with such a match is as a declared member \u2014 which the spec says is not a suspected duplicate (Cp35 `functionalityDeclaredHouseholdMemberNotFlagged`).\n\nThe household-size input to membership (`AssignHouseholdMemberCount`) already keyed off `householdId`, so with every owner now carrying a computed id it counts household members consistently \u2014 no formula change was needed (Cp23 stays at level 2).\n\nI also updated the OpenAPI descriptions/examples for `sharesHousehold`, `householdId`, and `identityKey`, and the create-pipeline comments in `owners.POST.yml`, to match the new behaviour.",
